WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.530
TV PRICES:
In our inflationary world filled with fiat

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:08.450
currencies and money printing, increasing
prices is simply a reality of daily life.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:12.429
Whether you’re trying to buy a house, a
cup of coffee, or a nice meal, you can bet

00:00:12.429 --> 00:00:17.920
that prices are only gonna go up over the
long term. In the 1960s for example, a big

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:26.090
mac only cost 45 cents. Today, a big mac costs
over 10 times that at $4.95. This trend is

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:30.560
especially true in the world of new tech where
companies are constantly trying to create

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:37.390
the next big thing and charge as much as possible.
However, in this universe of inflation, there

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:44.110
is one notable exception: TVs. Even if you
don’t follow TV prices too closely, you’ve

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:49.780
probably noticed TV prices creeping down whenever
you stroll by them at Costco or Best Buy.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:57.410
Nowadays, you can get 4K TVs for as little
as $200 and 85 inch 4K TVs for just $800 or

00:00:57.410 --> 00:01:02.500
for less than the price of a flagship smartphone.
Just 10 years ago, both of these would’ve

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:07.830
costed you thousands of dollars. But is this
just a case of new TV tech becoming cheaper

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:13.830
or are TV prices actually falling across the
board? Well, if you take a look at the historical

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:20.150
price of TVs, it becomes clear that it’s
very much the latter. Basically since 1950,

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:24.979
TVs have been consistently falling in prices.
There have been a few years here and there

00:01:24.979 --> 00:01:29.950
where they went up but most of those years
had inflation that was even higher. So, the

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:35.399
real prices of TVs were still going down.
This trend has only accelerated over the past

00:01:35.399 --> 00:01:41.160
20 years during which TVs have been consistently
falling by 15% or more every single year.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:46.240
At the same time, TVs have been consistently
getting bigger and better as well. At the

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:52.729
turn of the century, the average TV was only
24 inches. Today, the average TV is 50 inches.

00:01:52.729 --> 00:02:00.200
We’ve also gone from plasma TVs to LCDs
to LEDs to OLEDs, QLEDs, neo QLEDs, mini LEDS,

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:06.369
and who knows what else. Not to mention, resolutions
seem to be endlessly going up. So, somehow,

00:02:06.369 --> 00:02:12.280
we’re able to get bigger and better TVs
than ever for a lot cheaper than ever. So,

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:18.470
here’s the inexplicable economics of TVs
and how TV prices keep falling into a seemingly

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:21.260
endless abyss.

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:30.390
ADOPTION DELAY:
Taking a look back, the phenomenon of TVs

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:35.150
falling in prices can be traced all the way
back to the beginning of TVs themselves. Well,

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:40.530
technically, the technology used in TVs can
actually be traced back to the early 1800s,

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:46.470
but we’re more focused on modern commercial
TVs which take us back to September of 1928.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:50.730
This is when the first commercially produced
television set was shown off to the public.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:55.840
This, however, was terrible timing as the
Great Depression hit just one year later making

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:00.450
it extremely difficult to introduce new consumer
tech into the market. In fact, it would take

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:05.720
a full 10 years for this demonstration to
turn into a widely available product. But,

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:11.849
it was worth the wait given that it would
become a instant success, but things were

00:03:11.849 --> 00:03:15.870
just getting started. It wasn’t till after
World War 2 and after America entered a new

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:21.030
age of prosperity and economic growth that
people started splurging on homes, cars, and

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:27.100
of course TVs. But not only were TV sales
going to the moon, so was TV tech. Starting

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:33.230
in 1946, we would see a heated race between
CBS and RCA to bring color TVs into every

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:40.080
household. CBS would end up beating RCA to
market but not without making a massive sacrifice.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:44.000
The color television sets that CBS came up
with weren't compatible with the black and

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.799
white TV sets that millions of Americans already
had. Nonetheless, the FCC would declare CBS

00:03:49.799 --> 00:03:54.849
color TVs as being the new national standard.
Instead of fighting against the FCC on this

00:03:54.849 --> 00:04:00.470
decision though, RCA would simply continue
developing a more compatible color TV standard.

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:04.909
And the FCC would eventually concede that
RCA’s design was much more practical making

00:04:04.909 --> 00:04:12.209
them the new standard in 1953. It was a classic
case of the better invention coming out on

00:04:12.209 --> 00:04:19.490
top, but honestly, none of this really mattered.
It wasn’t till 13 years later in 1966 when

00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:24.400
color TV broadcasting became more popular
that people actually started buying color

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:30.500
TVs. And this brings us into the first unique
factor of the TV market which is that it has

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:33.240
always had a massive adoption delay. The first
commercial TV was created in the late 1920s

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.360
but it didn’t take off till the 1940s. The
first color TVs started popping up in the

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:41.230
late 40s and early 50s, but they didn’t
really gain steam till the late 60s. And it’s

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:46.740
the same case to this day. OLED for example
was created in the late 1980s and it first

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:51.660
started showing up in TVs in 2004. But, it’s
only now that the general public is buying

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:57.400
into OLED and even now, there’s still a
lot of room for growth. For whatever reason,

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:02.100
people are willing to be extremely patient
with TVs which is in stark contrast to any

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:07.100
other tech industry. Take smartphones for
example. 5G didn’t even start getting deployed

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:12.680
till 2018 and 2019, and it wasn’t till the
2020s that it was actually widely available.

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:19.030
Yet, by the end of 2022 itself, over a 100
million Americans or about a ⅓ of the country

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:24.710
already had 5G. With most tech industries,
people want the absolutely latest and greatest.

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:29.740
They’re often reluctant to buy something
even 2 or 3 generations old but it’s the

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:36.419
complete opposite with TVs. People are not
only willing to buy 5-7 year old tech but

00:05:36.419 --> 00:05:40.950
they’re also happy to diss on new tech.
Just 10 years ago, it was extremely popular

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:45.290
to hate on 4k. The common belief seemed to
be that you couldn’t even tell a difference

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:51.580
between 1080p and 4k and that 4k was waste
of money. That was until 4k became dirt cheap

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:56.390
and people completely forgot about their concerns
and picked up a 4k TV. The same thing is happening

00:05:56.390 --> 00:06:02.280
with 8k as we speak. Who even needs 8k? You
can’t even tell the difference. 8k is so

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:07.750
stupid. There’s not even any 8k content
and on and on and on. It’s actually crazy

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:13.750
how heated people get over new TV tech. The
reality is that if you have an 85 or 98 inch

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:19.840
TV which are increasingly becoming the norm,
you can tell a difference between 4k and 8k.

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:25.380
Just not a difference that’s worth an extra
3 to $4000. If you give it 5 to 10 years though

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:30.410
and wait for 8k TV prices to become comparable
to 4k TV prices, and you can bet that the

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:36.470
general public will forget all about their
concerns and buy an 8k TV. And honestly that’s

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:41.160
great. People are happy to buy the new TV
tech when its offered at a reasonable price.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:46.440
But until then, they’re somehow completely
immune to marketing hype. If consumers approached

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:51.730
every industry with this mindset, credit card
debt wouldn’t even be thing, but unfortunately,

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:56.139
this magical consumer clarity only applies
to the TV industry which brings us into the

00:06:56.139 --> 00:07:01.599
question of why?

00:07:01.599 --> 00:07:08.660
CONSUMER CLARITY:
Trying to answer why consumers aren’t moved

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:14.350
by TV marketing is a rather tough question
to answer. The obvious explanation is that

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:19.740
TVs are a matured market that isn’t progressing
all that quickly. So, it’s easy for consumers

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:25.550
to settle on something that’s a bit older
but that’s not really true. There’s quite

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:31.509
a big difference between regular LED TVs and
OLED TVs. Definitely a much bigger difference

00:07:31.509 --> 00:07:37.230
than the iPhone 12 vs the iPhone 15. Yet,
I feel that it would be much easier to convince

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:42.530
someone to hold back and buy an LED TV than
it would be to get them to buy an iPhone 12.

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:47.560
And it’s not just with smartphones either.
I could maket the same argument with cars.

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:51.550
Assuming that all prices were the same, it
would be much harder to convince someone to

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:57.580
buy a 10 year old car than buy an LED TV,
and cars don’t even change. And this brings

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:04.111
me into one of the most basic marketing rules:
features don’t sell products. Automakers

00:08:04.111 --> 00:08:10.970
and Apple understand this perfectly. TV makers
do not. For decades, TV makers have been trying

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:17.060
to push TVs based on new tech whether that
be OLED or 8k or whatever. Consumers aren’t

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:22.400
really moved by features though. They’re
moved by apparent change. For example, unless

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:27.720
your a TV nerd, you probably don’t know
the difference between OLED, QLED, neo QLED,

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:33.440
and mini LED. But, regardless of how knowledgeable
you are on TVs, I bet you remember when TVs

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:39.310
went from being boxes to being flat or when
they went from being 24 inches to being 85

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:43.940
inches. These sort of changes may not actually
make that big of a difference to picture quality

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:51.000
compared to LED vs OLED. In fact, bigger TVs
usually have worse picture quality. But, nonetheless,

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:56.120
they’re often much more compelling reasons
to upgrade. If everyone around you has upgraded

00:08:56.120 --> 00:09:02.029
from box TVs to flat screen TVs or from 55
inch TVs to 85 inch TVs, you’d probably

00:09:02.029 --> 00:09:07.860
wanna upgrade as well. This is precisely the
formula that automakers leverage. Every couple

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:11.890
of years, automakers will change up the design
of their cars just to keep things feeling

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:16.950
fresh and new. This way, by the time your
current car is 10 to 15 years old, even if

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:22.360
it’s working perfectly fine, it’ll feel
old and outdated. It’s the same case with

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:27.140
Apple. With every generation, they try to
add something that makes it distinctly clear

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:32.330
that it’s the new one. For example, iPhones
that still have home buttons definitely feel

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:37.120
outdated. iPhones with notches as opposed
to dynamic islands are even starting to feel

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:43.279
outdated despite it being a non material difference.
At this point, there’s not much more that

00:09:43.279 --> 00:09:47.500
can change with the iPhone’s design and
that’s why Apple is dragging those out for

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:52.560
as long as possible. Things like removing
the dynamic island or camera bump altogether.

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:57.810
With TVs, on the other hand, they’ve basically
always been in their final form. It’s only

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:02.420
a few times in history that the design has
fundamentally changed at which points people

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:08.160
did upgrade quickly. But putting those instances
aside, there’s really no obvious difference

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:14.310
between any of the new TV standards. Sure,
they’re better and you want it, but unless

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:19.310
you put them side by side, you wouldn’t
even notice a difference. So, people are able

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:23.899
to be surprisingly patient when it comes to
new TV tech and they don’t particularly

00:10:23.899 --> 00:10:29.270
feel left behind or left out either leading
to astounding consumer clarity. But while

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:39.070
this is great for consumers, it sucks for
TV makers leading to some pretty odd solutions.

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:46.209
FORCING CHANGE:
Given this consumer sentiment, TV makers have

00:10:46.209 --> 00:10:50.880
been trying to introduce material changes
as regularly as possible but most of this

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:55.779
efforts end up falling flat. For example,
remember that time that TVs went from being

00:10:55.779 --> 00:11:01.890
flat to being curved to being flat again?
Or what about that time that 3d TVs were supposedly

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:06.630
the next big thing. The reality is that it’s
extremely difficult to force changes like

00:11:06.630 --> 00:11:12.050
these unless you’re Apple and over the years,
TV makers have come to accept this reality.

00:11:12.050 --> 00:11:16.269
Instead, they’ve turned their attention
to alternative modes of monetization like

00:11:16.269 --> 00:11:20.660
data collection. It turns out that you’re
viewing habits are extremely valuable to streaming

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:26.050
services like Netflix and Disney+. So, TV
makers no longer even need to make money when

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:31.579
you buy the TV. Instead, they make their money
in the following 5-10 years selling your data

00:11:31.579 --> 00:11:38.140
to streaming giants. In fact, the average
margin across the TV industry is only 6% and

00:11:38.140 --> 00:11:43.390
that’s with the stupid expensive 8k OLED
TVs pulling that margin up. So, if you buy

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:48.459
a regular TV with a strong discount on Black
Friday or Christmas, there’s a good chance

00:11:48.459 --> 00:11:55.820
that you got that TV at cost or even under
cost. Larger brands like LG and Samsung don’t

00:11:55.820 --> 00:12:00.630
even care all that much about post purchase
monetization either. For them, the TV industry

00:12:00.630 --> 00:12:05.860
has become something that they use to simply
maximize brand presence and dominance as opposed

00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:12.000
to really making money. So, why do TV prices
keep going down? Well, you’ve of course

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:16.770
got all of your classic stuff like economies
of scale, the commoditization of TVs, new

00:12:16.770 --> 00:12:22.020
tech making old tech cheaper, and all that
jazz. But the real bottom line is that the

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:28.470
general public doesn’t buy into TV marketing
hype. So, after TV makers burn through the

00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:32.420
rich buyers who can afford to burn a couple
grand, they’re forced to quickly decrease

00:12:32.420 --> 00:12:37.810
prices to appeal to a much broader audience.
Some TV makers have figured out alternative

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:43.570
modes of monetization but for the most part,
TVs have become a near 0 margin industry that

00:12:43.570 --> 00:12:50.370
companies just use to stay relevant and omnipresent
resulting in TV prices falling forever. Samsung

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:54.420
is starting to feel this same squeeze within
the smartphone industry as we enter the era

00:12:54.420 --> 00:13:00.749
of peak smartphone. Check out this video to
learn more.

